THOUGHT: `parentMap` is explicitly annotated `@Nullable`, but `ILayer.getMap()` has a non-null contract. Other methods already guard against `parentMap == null` by returning defaults. To satisfy NullAway and the API contract, `getMap()` should never return null; throwing an exception when `parentMap` is unexpectedly null is safer than silently returning null. I’ll update `getMap()` to check for null and throw an `IllegalStateException` if `parentMap` is not set. Then I’ll run the build to confirm the NullAway error count decreases by one and no new errors appear.

```bash
cd /home/vscode/nullness-benchmarks/litiengine && sed -i '156,160c\  @Override
  public IMap getMap() {
    if (this.parentMap == null) {
      throw new IllegalStateException("Layer is not attached to a map");
    }

    return this.parentMap;
  }' src/de/gurkenlabs/litiengine/environment/tilemap/xml/Layer.java && ./gradlew compileJava -x test -q > new_build_output.log 2>&1 && grep -E "NullAway: .*errors" -n initial_build_output.log new_build_output.log
```

